  <!--
    ImpactAssessment : ImpactAssessmentType : ImpactAssessmentBaseType : platform:StatusLineBaseType

    ImpactAssessmentType : ImpactAssessmentBaseType                        | ImpactAssessment Usage
    .......................................................................|......................................................
        Extension: ImpactAssessmentExtensionType  [0..*]                   | for data not specified in agcXML

    ImpactAssessmentBaseType : platform:StatusLineBaseType                 | ImpactAssessment Usage
    .......................................................................|......................................................
        ImpactCode           : ImpactCodeType              [0..*]          | type of impact
        DescriptionAttachment: platform:AttachmentType     [0..*]          | assessment as an attachment
        Amount               : platform:OpenAmountType     [0..*]          | amount of money
        Duration             : platform:OpenDurationType   [0..*]          | amount of time
        DateTime             : platform:OpenDateTimeType   [0..*]          | specific date and/or time
        Time                 : platform:OpenTimeType       [0..*]          | time without date
        Code                 : platform:OpenCodeType       [0..*]          | general purpose codelist value
        ID                   : platform:OpenIDType         [0..*]          | general purpose identifier
        Indicator            : platform:OpenIndicatorType  [0..*]          | general purpose indicator
        Measure              : platform:OpenMeasureType    [0..*]          | general purpose measurement such as weight
        Number               : platform:OpenNumberType     [0..*]          | general purpose numeric value
        Percent              : platform:OpenPercentType    [0..*]          | general purpose percentage value
        Quantity             : platform:OpenQuantityType   [0..*]          | general purpose quantity

    platform:StatusLineBaseType                                            | ImpactAssessment Usage
    .......................................................................|......................................................
        platform:LineNumberID     : platform:OpenIDType             [0..1] | line number of this impact assessment
        platform:LineIDSet        : platform:IDSetType              [0..*] | composite line number, if needed
        platform:Description      : platform:DescriptionType        [0..*] | assessment in plain text
        platform:Note             : platform:NoteType               [0..*] | discussion among RFI participants on this assessment
        platform:DocumentReference: platform:DocumentReferenceType  [0..*] | references to external documents for this assessment
        platform:Attachment       : platform:AttachmentType         [0..*] | inline attachment of documents for this assessment
        platform:Status           : platform:StatusType             [0..*] | 
  -->
  <xs:element name="ImpactAssessment" type="ImpactAssessmentType">
    <xs:annotation>
      <xs:documentation>An assessment of the impact for a change in workflow, such as RFI, on a construction project.  Examples include cost, schedule, drawing and environmental impacts.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>Depending on the stage of the workflow, the impact could be expected, suggested, proposed, constrained or final.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>An impact assessment includes an impact code, narratives, attachments and supporting dtata.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>For example, a cost impact would use ImpactCode=Cost and the Amount field.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>A schedule impact would use ImpactCode=Schedule and the Duration, DateTime and Time fields as needed.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>A drawing impact would use ImpactCode=Drawing and the platform:Attachment field.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>There are other impact codes and custom impact codes are allowed.  Generally, each impact code uses the appropriate fields.  In addition, any impact assessment can include notes (including author and timestamp), free form descriptions, attachments and references.  To aid automation, numbers, dates, amounts and other data mentioned in the free form text can be included in corresponding fields.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>All field types are the general purpose "Open" variety in that they carry a "typeCode" that parties can agree upon to find very specific uses for general concepts of amounts, dates, durations, etc.</xs:documentation>
    </xs:annotation>
  </xs:element>
  <xs:complexType name="ImpactAssessmentType">
    <xs:annotation>
      <xs:documentation>An assessment of the impact for a change in workflow, such as RFI, on a construction project.  Examples include cost, schedule, drawing and environmental impacts.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>Depending on the stage of the workflow, the impact could be expected, suggested, proposed, constrained or final.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>An impact assessment includes an impact code, narratives, attachments and supporting dtata.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>For example, a cost impact would use ImpactCode=Cost and the Amount field.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>A schedule impact would use ImpactCode=Schedule and the Duration, DateTime and Time fields as needed.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>A drawing impact would use ImpactCode=Drawing and the platform:Attachment field.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>There are other impact codes and custom impact codes are allowed.  Generally, each impact code uses the appropriate fields.  In addition, any impact assessment can include notes (including author and timestamp), free form descriptions, attachments and references.  To aid automation, numbers, dates, amounts and other data mentioned in the free form text can be included in corresponding fields.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>All field types are the general purpose "Open" variety in that they carry a "typeCode" that parties can agree upon to find very specific uses for general concepts of amounts, dates, durations, etc.</xs:documentation>
    </xs:annotation>
    <xs:complexContent>
      <xs:extension base="ImpactAssessmentBaseType">
        <xs:sequence>
          <xs:element name="Extension" type="ImpactAssessmentExtensionType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
            <xs:annotation>
              <xs:documentation>Allows additional information not captured in the agcXML specification.</xs:documentation>
              <xs:documentation>To build an extension, define a new XML schema in a separate XML namespace.</xs:documentation>
              <xs:documentation>To use an extension, import your new  schema into the XML instance for a BOD and add fields to an Extension field. Extension fields can be found in many nouns, headers, components and fields.</xs:documentation>
            </xs:annotation>
          </xs:element>
        </xs:sequence>
      </xs:extension>
    </xs:complexContent>
  </xs:complexType>
  <xs:complexType name="ImpactAssessmentBaseType">
    <xs:annotation>
      <xs:documentation>A base type used to define ImpactAssessmentType.  All content for ImpactAssessmentType is defined here except for ImpactAssessmentType.Extension.</xs:documentation>
    </xs:annotation>
    <xs:complexContent>
      <xs:extension base="platform:StatusLineBaseType">
        <xs:sequence>
          <xs:element ref="ImpactCode" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
            <xs:annotation>
              <xs:documentation>Type of impact on a construction project.  Used to assess a change in a workflow such as RFI.</xs:documentation>
              <xs:documentation>Enumerated values are:  Cost, Schedule, Drawing, PlanOrSpecification, Environmental.  This is an open code list in agcXML.</xs:documentation>
            </xs:annotation>
          </xs:element>
          <xs:element name="DescriptionAttachment" type="platform:AttachmentType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
            <xs:annotation>
              <xs:documentation>Statement of the impact assessment using an attached document.  This document might contain numbers and other data such as dates.  If so, consider placing the values in the data fields as well to facilitate automated analysis.</xs:documentation>
              <xs:documentation>Alternatively, the entire statement of the assessment can be made using free form text with field platform:Description.</xs:documentation>
              <xs:documentation>Fields platform:Description and DescriptionAttachment can be used together or as alternatives.</xs:documentation>
            </xs:annotation>
          </xs:element>
          <xs:element name="Amount" type="platform:OpenAmountType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
            <xs:annotation>
              <xs:documentation>Amount of money.  Used, for example, for a cost impact.</xs:documentation>
            </xs:annotation>
          </xs:element>
          <xs:element name="Duration" type="platform:OpenDurationType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
            <xs:annotation>
              <xs:documentation>Amount of time.  Used, for example, for a schedule impact.</xs:documentation>
            </xs:annotation>
          </xs:element>
          <xs:element name="DateTime" type="platform:OpenDateTimeType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
            <xs:annotation>
              <xs:documentation>A date or a point in time on a specific date.  Used, for example, for a schedule impact.</xs:documentation>
            </xs:annotation>
          </xs:element>
          <xs:element name="Time" type="platform:OpenTimeType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
            <xs:annotation>
              <xs:documentation>A point in time without a date attached.  Used, for example, for a schedule impact.</xs:documentation>
            </xs:annotation>
          </xs:element>
          <xs:element name="Code" type="platform:OpenCodeType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
            <xs:annotation>
              <xs:documentation>A general purpose, open codelist value.  Used, for example, for a LEED credit reference in an in environmental impact.</xs:documentation>
            </xs:annotation>
          </xs:element>
          <xs:element name="ID" type="platform:OpenIDType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
            <xs:annotation>
              <xs:documentation>A general purpose identifier.  Distinct from the platform:LineNumberID field used to identify this impact assessment.</xs:documentation>
            </xs:annotation>
          </xs:element>
          <xs:element name="Indicator" type="platform:OpenIndicatorType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
            <xs:annotation>
              <xs:documentation>A general purpose true/false indicator.</xs:documentation>
            </xs:annotation>
          </xs:element>
          <xs:element name="Measure" type="platform:OpenMeasureType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
            <xs:annotation>
              <xs:documentation>A numeric measurement such as weight or distance.</xs:documentation>
            </xs:annotation>
          </xs:element>
          <xs:element name="Number" type="platform:OpenNumberType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
            <xs:annotation>
              <xs:documentation>A general purprose number.</xs:documentation>
            </xs:annotation>
          </xs:element>
          <xs:element name="Percent" type="platform:OpenPercentType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
            <xs:annotation>
              <xs:documentation>A general purprose percentage.</xs:documentation>
            </xs:annotation>
          </xs:element>
          <xs:element name="Quantity" type="platform:OpenQuantityType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
            <xs:annotation>
              <xs:documentation>A counted number of non-monetary units, possibly not a whole number.</xs:documentation>
            </xs:annotation>
          </xs:element>
        </xs:sequence>
      </xs:extension>
    </xs:complexContent>
  </xs:complexType>

  <!--
    Participant : ParticipantType

    ParticipantType                                  | Participant Usage
    .................................................|......................................................
        ID             : platform:IDType      [0..1] | id for the participant
        ProjectContact : ProjectContactType   [0..*] | contact info for someone in the construction project
        SchemeAgencyUse: SchemeAgencyUseType  [0..*] | id or id prefix of scheme agency id used
  -->
  <xs:element name="Participant" type="ParticipantType">
    <xs:annotation>
      <xs:documentation>A single participant in a workflow process such as RFI.  Each is a business party that has a specific role in a construction project as as GC or Architect.  However, an outside authority with oversight such as a governmental highway authority can also be a participant.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>Each participant also has a contribution to the workflow such as responding to assigned work, marking remarks, adding attachments and issuing receipts for documents received.  Contributions are indicated elsewhere in a BOD by making a simple reference to the Participant/ID field.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>Each participant can also declare scheme agency ids or root prefixes for scheme agency ids.  This facility enables the pairing an id used elsewhere in the BOD with a specific participant.</xs:documentation>
    </xs:annotation>
  </xs:element>
  <xs:complexType name="ParticipantType">
    <xs:annotation>
      <xs:documentation>A single participant in a workflow process such as RFI.  Each is a business party that has a specific role in a construction project as as GC or Architect.  However, an outside authority with oversight such as a governmental highway authority can also be a participant.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>Each participant also has a contribution to the workflow such as responding to assigned work, marking remarks, adding attachments and issuing receipts for documents received.  Contributions are indicated elsewhere in a BOD by making a simple reference to the Participant/ID field.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>Each participant can also declare scheme agency ids or root prefixes for scheme agency ids.  This facility enables the pairing an id used elsewhere in the BOD with a specific participant.</xs:documentation>
    </xs:annotation>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:element name="ID" type="platform:IDType" minOccurs="0">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>A id for a &lt;Participant&gt; for reference by other elements or attributes within the same BOD instance.  As such, id need only be unique among other &lt;Participant&gt; items within the same BOD instance.  The id need not have meaning outside the BOD instance in which it appears.</xs:documentation>
          <xs:documentation>The value of the id, not counting any attributes, must be usable as a key by the &lt;xs:key&gt; facility in XML Schema.  In agcXML, all &lt;xs:key&gt; and &lt;xs:keyref&gt; declarations appear in the Noun's &lt;xs:element&gt; due to restrictions in XML Schema.  These restrictions require declarations only in an &lt;xs:element&gt;, and the &lt;xs:key&gt; has to be at or above any key references.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:element>
      <xs:element ref="ProjectContact" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>A contact for someone in a construction project including the role(s) on the project.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:element>
      <xs:element ref="SchemeAgencyUse" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>A declaration that scheme agency identifier is used.  For example, a Participant can declare its scheme agency ids. Optionally, this can be a root prefix of schema agency ids used.</xs:documentation>
          <xs:documentation>The intent is to be able to associate an ID that appears elsewhere in the BOD with the usage, such as by a Participant.  When this is a root prefix, then any ID that begins with this prefix is associated with this usage.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:element>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>

  <xs:element name="Participants" type="ParticipantsType">
    <xs:annotation>
      <xs:documentation>A reference list for the potentially many participants in a workflow process such as RFI.  Each is a business party that has a specific role in a construction project as as GC or Architect.  However, an outside authority with oversight such as a governmental highway authority can also be a participant.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>Each participant also has a contribution to the workflow such as responding to assigned work, marking remarks, adding attachments and issuing receipts for documents received.  Contributions are indicated elsewhere in a BOD by making a simple reference to the Participant/ID field.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>Each participant can also declare scheme agency ids or root prefixes for scheme agency ids.  This facility enables the pairing an id used elsewhere in the BOD with a specific participant.</xs:documentation>
    </xs:annotation>
    <xs:unique name="ParticipantID_Unique">
      <xs:annotation>
        <xs:documentation>Participant-specific ids must be unique across all participants</xs:documentation>
      </xs:annotation>
      <xs:selector xpath="gc:Participant"></xs:selector>
      <xs:field xpath="gc:ID"></xs:field>
    </xs:unique>
    <xs:unique name="ParticipantSchemeAgencyUse_Unique">
      <xs:annotation>
        <xs:documentation>Participant-specific ids for scheme agency must be unique across all participants</xs:documentation>
      </xs:annotation>
      <xs:selector xpath="gc:Participant/gc:SchemeAgencyUse"></xs:selector>
      <xs:field xpath="."></xs:field>
    </xs:unique>
  </xs:element>
  <xs:complexType name="ParticipantsType">
    <xs:annotation>
      <xs:documentation>A reference list for the potentially many participants in a workflow process such as RFI.  Each is a business party that has a specific role in a construction project as as GC or Architect.  However, an outside authority with oversight such as a governmental highway authority can also be a participant.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>Each participant also has a contribution to the workflow such as responding to assigned work, marking remarks, adding attachments and issuing receipts for documents received.  Contributions are indicated elsewhere in a BOD by making a simple reference to the Participant/ID field.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>Each participant can also declare scheme agency ids or root prefixes for scheme agency ids.  This facility enables the pairing an id used elsewhere in the BOD with a specific participant.</xs:documentation>
    </xs:annotation>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:element name="Participant" type="ParticipantType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>A single participant in a workflow process such as RFI.  Each is a business party that has a specific role in a construction project as as GC or Architect.  However, an outside authority with oversight such as a governmental highway authority can also be a participant.</xs:documentation>
          <xs:documentation>Each participant also has a contribution to the workflow such as responding to assigned work, marking remarks, adding attachments and issuing receipts for documents received.  Contributions are indicated elsewhere in a BOD by making a simple reference to the Participant/ID field.</xs:documentation>
          <xs:documentation>Each participant can also declare scheme agency ids or root prefixes for scheme agency ids.  This facility enables the pairing an id used elsewhere in the BOD with a specific participant.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:element>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>

  <!--
    Project : ProjectType

    ProjectType                                       | Project Usage
    ..................................................|....................................
        Number     : platform:IDType           [0..1] | project number used by all parties
        NumberSet  : platform:IDSetType        [0..1] | composite project number
        Title      : platform:DescriptionType  [0..1] | title of construction project
        SiteAddress: platform:AddressType      [0..*] | address of construction site
  -->
  <xs:element name="Project" type="ProjectType">
    <xs:annotation>
      <xs:documentation>Reference to the construction project.  A project number is probably sufficient.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>Details of the project definition are defined elsewhere.  It is expected that any party that needs project details can use this project number to retrieve them.</xs:documentation>
    </xs:annotation>
  </xs:element>
  <xs:complexType name="ProjectType">
    <xs:annotation>
      <xs:documentation>Reference to the construction project.  A project number is probably sufficient.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>Details of the project definition are defined elsewhere.  It is expected that any party that needs project details can use this project number to retrieve them.</xs:documentation>
    </xs:annotation>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:element name="Number" type="platform:IDType" minOccurs="0">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Construction project number, often issued by the general contractor</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:element>
      <xs:element name="NumberSet" type="platform:IDSetType" minOccurs="0">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Composite construction project number, often issued by the general contractor.</xs:documentation>
          <xs:documentation>This field is used instead of Number when a composite identifier is needed.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:element>
      <xs:element name="Title" type="platform:DescriptionType" minOccurs="0">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Title of the project for reports and quick identification by participants</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:element>
      <xs:element name="SiteAddress" type="platform:AddressType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Address of the construction site</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:element>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>

  <!--
    ProjectContact : ProjectContactType : ProjectContactBaseType : platform:ContactBaseType

    ProjectContactType : ProjectContactBaseType                                                  | ProjectContact Usage
    .............................................................................................|.......................................
        Extension: ProjectContactExtensionType  [0..*]                                           | for data not specified in agcXML

    ProjectContactBaseType : platform:ContactBaseType                                            | ProjectContact Usage
    .............................................................................................|.......................................
        ProjectRoleCode: ProjectRoleCodeType  [1..*]                                             | role in project such as Architect

    platform:ContactBaseType                                                                     | ProjectContact Usage
    .............................................................................................|.......................................
        platform:EffectiveTimePeriod         : platform:TimePeriodType                    [0..1] | when the contact was active
        platform:PersonName                  : platform:PersonNameType                    [0..*] | full naming details
        platform:JobTitle                    : platform:TextType                          [0..1] | job position or title
        platform:Responsibility              : platform:TextType                          [0..*] | description of job responsibility
        platform:OrganizationUnitID          : platform:IDType                            [0..1] | 
        platform:Communication               : platform:CommunicationType                 [0..*] | general purpose communication details
        platform:TelephoneCommunication      : platform:TelephoneCommunicationType        [0..*] | telephone details
        platform:EMailAddressCommunication   : platform:EMailAddressCommunicationType     [0..*] | email details
        platform:InternetAddressCommunication: platform:InternetAddressCommunicationType  [0..*] | web details
        platform:Preference                  : platform:PreferenceType                    [0..1] | preferences such as availability
  -->
  <xs:element name="ProjectContact" type="ProjectContactType">
    <xs:annotation>
      <xs:documentation>A contact for someone in a construction project including the role(s) on the project.</xs:documentation>
    </xs:annotation>
  </xs:element>
  <xs:complexType name="ProjectContactType">
    <xs:annotation>
      <xs:documentation>A contact for someone in a construction project including the role(s) on the project.</xs:documentation>
    </xs:annotation>
    <xs:complexContent>
      <xs:extension base="ProjectContactBaseType">
        <xs:sequence>
          <xs:element name="Extension" type="ProjectContactExtensionType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
            <xs:annotation>
              <xs:documentation>Allows additional information not captured in the agcXML specification.</xs:documentation>
              <xs:documentation>To build an extension, define a new XML schema in a separate XML namespace.</xs:documentation>
              <xs:documentation>To use an extension, import your new  schema into the XML instance for a BOD and add fields to an Extension field. Extension fields can be found in many nouns, headers, components and fields.</xs:documentation>
            </xs:annotation>
          </xs:element>
        </xs:sequence>
      </xs:extension>
    </xs:complexContent>
  </xs:complexType>
  <xs:complexType name="ProjectContactBaseType">
    <xs:annotation>
      <xs:documentation>A base type used to define ProjectContactType.  All content for ProjectContactType is defined here except for ProjectContactType.Extension.</xs:documentation>
    </xs:annotation>
    <xs:complexContent>
      <xs:extension base="platform:ContactBaseType">
        <xs:sequence>
          <xs:element ref="ProjectRoleCode" maxOccurs="unbounded">
            <xs:annotation>
              <xs:documentation>Role of a participant in the project such as a contractor or owner.</xs:documentation>
              <xs:documentation>Enumerated values are:  PrimeArchitect, Architect, PrimeDesigner, Designer, Owner, OwnerRepresentative, PrimeContractor, Contractor, Subcontractor, NotaryRepresentative, BrokerRepresentative, SuretyRepresentative, ConstructionManager, ConstructionManagerConstructorRepresentative, ConstructionManagerAdvisorRepresentative, ContractorSafetyRepresentative, ContractorRepresentative, EngineerRepresentative, ArchitectRepresentative, ExternalAuthority.  This is an open code list in agcXML.</xs:documentation>
            </xs:annotation>
          </xs:element>
        </xs:sequence>
      </xs:extension>
    </xs:complexContent>
  </xs:complexType>

  <!--
    Receipt : ReceiptType

    ReceiptType                        | Receipt Usage
    ...................................|...............
        platform:FreeFormTextGroup:    | 
  -->
  <xs:element name="Receipt" type="ReceiptType">
    <xs:annotation>
      <xs:documentation>A workflow, such as  RFI, can include confirmed tracking for recipients, such as a governmental highway authority, that legally must receive copies of the final documents.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>Much like a CC list on an email in concept, the list of receipts show who has received these final copies.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>Unlike the CC list on an email, though, the evidence supports non-repudiation needed in a legal setting when years later after a road construction project completes, a lawyer investigates the history of work done (RFIs) to see who is responsible for a law suit.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>A receipt here is not a suggested or best effort list like a CC on an email is.  Rather, a receipt is a document reference or attachment such as a signed PDF from the recipient affirming appropriate procedures.  A reference to a non-electronic document can also be used.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>This approach allows whatever mechanism is needed to sustain a legal investigation to be part of the electronic record.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>The receipt also serves as a placeholder while a workflow (RFI) is in progress for an "intended receipt."  Here, the analogy is closer to a CC list while the work is still in progress.  The intended receipt also has value after the work completes if the intent could not be fulfilled.  The intended receipt documents completion but that a receipt could not be obtained; while not obtaining a receipt has legal issues, the emphasis here is that the state of receipt collection can be part of the record.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>It is possible that the receipt is just a type of attachment or reference rather than a distinguished field.  Other types of authorization are needed in other parts of agcXML such as signatories on a change order.  These signatories also have to withstand legal evidence scruitiny.  It is unlikely that the mere existence of an electronic transaction will be used as the evidence.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>The value of having such a reference here is that it gives party B the electronic opportunity to download and examine the evidence.  Otherwise, an out-of-band communication is required that would slow down the business process unnecessarily.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>While it is not required that a referenced document be downloadable through automation, it is allowed.  This means that the business parties can streamline the buiness process to the level of their comfort.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>To be more specific, party A might not be willing to make all of its legally binding documents downloadable.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>TODO:  Receipts are currently being modeled while agcXML is an Alpha.  For now, an open content model is used.</xs:documentation>
    </xs:annotation>
  </xs:element>
  <xs:complexType name="ReceiptType">
    <xs:annotation>
      <xs:documentation>A workflow, such as  RFI, can include confirmed tracking for recipients, such as a governmental highway authority, that legally must receive copies of the final documents.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>Much like a CC list on an email in concept, the list of receipts show who has received these final copies.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>Unlike the CC list on an email, though, the evidence supports non-repudiation needed in a legal setting when years later after a road construction project completes, a lawyer investigates the history of work done (RFIs) to see who is responsible for a law suit.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>A receipt here is not a suggested or best effort list like a CC on an email is.  Rather, a receipt is a document reference or attachment such as a signed PDF from the recipient affirming appropriate procedures.  A reference to a non-electronic document can also be used.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>This approach allows whatever mechanism is needed to sustain a legal investigation to be part of the electronic record.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>The receipt also serves as a placeholder while a workflow (RFI) is in progress for an "intended receipt."  Here, the analogy is closer to a CC list while the work is still in progress.  The intended receipt also has value after the work completes if the intent could not be fulfilled.  The intended receipt documents completion but that a receipt could not be obtained; while not obtaining a receipt has legal issues, the emphasis here is that the state of receipt collection can be part of the record.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>It is possible that the receipt is just a type of attachment or reference rather than a distinguished field.  Other types of authorization are needed in other parts of agcXML such as signatories on a change order.  These signatories also have to withstand legal evidence scruitiny.  It is unlikely that the mere existence of an electronic transaction will be used as the evidence.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>The value of having such a reference here is that it gives party B the electronic opportunity to download and examine the evidence.  Otherwise, an out-of-band communication is required that would slow down the business process unnecessarily.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>While it is not required that a referenced document be downloadable through automation, it is allowed.  This means that the business parties can streamline the buiness process to the level of their comfort.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>To be more specific, party A might not be willing to make all of its legally binding documents downloadable.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>TODO:  Receipts are currently being modeled while agcXML is an Alpha.  For now, an open content model is used.</xs:documentation>
    </xs:annotation>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:group ref="platform:FreeFormTextGroup">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation></xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:group>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>

